            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Alert Fired, But the Campaign Still Stopped</h2>
<p>This is a common frustration for overseas marketing teams: an alert arrives at 3 AM, but by morning the account has been suspended for hours. For an e-commerce site spending $10,000+ daily, that's a significant loss. The problem usually isn't that alerts weren't set up—it's that the alert mechanism has hidden flaws. Here are the key details to check.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: Is Your Threshold Based on Total Balance or Hourly Spend Rate?</h2>
<p>Many teams set fixed dollar thresholds, like "alert when balance drops below $500." This seems reasonable, but if the account spends $1,000 daily, $500 means only half a day of budget—barely enough time to process a recharge.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Thresholds should cover at least 3-6 hours of theoretical spend while allowing buffer time for recharge processing. For a $2,000/day account, set alerts at "balance below $1,500 OR projected balance insufficient for 24 hours of spend"—either condition triggers notification.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: Who Receives the Alert Notifications?</h2>
<p>The account admin is in one group chat, the performance marketer in another, and finance in a third. The alert goes to the admin's email, but they didn't check it over the weekend.</p>
<p><strong>Direct advice</strong>: Alerts must reach the person actually monitoring the account, through at least two channels (email + instant messaging). For high-spend accounts, have both the marketer and finance receive alerts to avoid single points of failure.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: Is Auto-Recharge Set Up, and What Are the Triggers?</h2>
<p>Manual recharges depend on human response speed—auto-recharge is the real safety net. But many teams set auto-recharge rules too conservatively, like "trigger recharge when balance is below $100." By that point, it's already too late.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended logic</strong>: Auto-recharge triggers should be higher than alert thresholds. For example, if alerts trigger at "projected balance insufficient for 48 hours," auto-recharge should trigger at "projected balance insufficient for 36 hours." This provides buffer even if processing takes time.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: For Multi-Account Management, Does Each Sub-Account Have Its Own Alert?</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team operates accounts in the US, UK, and Southeast Asia, managed by the same team. They only set alerts for the main account. The Southeast Asia account ran dry for three days before anyone noticed—it was the smallest spender, so it wasn't monitored closely.</p>
<p><strong>Standard</strong>: Every advertising account should have independent alert rules. Thresholds can vary by account spend size, but every account must have alerts configured. Don't focus only on high-spend accounts and neglect smaller ones.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: For Slow-Reload Situations, Does the Alert Have Enough Lead Time?</h2>
<p>Some overseas ad platforms (particularly certain Google Ads account types or non-DSA-managed Meta accounts) may have 1-4 hour delays in fund processing. If alerts give only 10 minutes advance notice, there's no time to act once someone sees it.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Set alerts more aggressively in the advance timing. If normal recharges take 2 hours to process, alerts should fire at least 3 hours early. For critical accounts, add "pending recharge" and "recharge triggered" status indicators so the team knows exactly where things stand.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Checklist: Does Your Alert Mechanism Have These Gaps?</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Thresholds based on projected spend days, not fixed dollar amounts</li>
<li>[ ] Alert notifications sent to actual executors, not just admins</li>
<li>[ ] Auto-reload triggers set higher than alert thresholds</li>
<li>[ ] All sub-accounts have independent alerts, with no gaps</li>
<li>[ ] Alert lead time covers expected recharge processing delays</li>
<li>[ ] Established SOP for alert response (who acts, within what timeframe)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>The core of ad account balance management isn't just "having notifications"—it's whether you can respond quickly after receiving one. Setting up alerts is only the first step. Establishing a complete closed loop from alert to recharge is what truly prevents campaign disruption.</p>
